[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003188","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003188","source_record_id":"rec_003188","source_corpus":"include","title":"Multimodal Cell Context Instruction Tuning for Conditional DNA Regulatory Sequence Generation with Large Language Models","abstract":"Designing biologically plausible regulatory sequences, such as enhancers, is a key challenge in synthetic biology. Current approaches either classify active enhancers or generate synthetic sequences independently but often fail to address the need for cell-type-specific recruitment of multiple transcription factors (TFs) for effective transcription activation. This oversight leads to suboptimal designs. To overcome these limitations, we propose LEONINE, a novel framework redefining enhancer sequence design as a multimodal question-answering task within a cellular context. LEONINE utilizes large language models (LLMs) trained on DNA sequences and integrates multimodal cellular data-including promoter sequences, gene expression profiles, and cell type information-to optimize enhancer sequences for specific regulatory environments. A large-scale dataset of over 1.5 million cell-type-specific promoter-enhancer pairs and a robust evaluation benchmark were developed to support this effort. Extensive evaluations across seven cell types demonstrate that LEONINE outperforms state-of-the-art LLMs, generating biologically and functionally coherent sequences. This work introduces a new paradigm for context-aware DNA sequence design, advancing research in synthetic biology and gene regulation.\n\n##### Index Terms\n\nMultimodal Large Language Models; Regulatory Sequence Generation; Discrete Signals","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source: 3. EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS > 3.1. Experimental Setup]\nTo effectively benchmark our model, we adopted the cell type-specific promoter-enhancer pairs from the PsychENCODE consortium [18]. Using their published scATAC-seq and scRNA-seq dataset, we identified distal peak-to-gene linkages for seven major cell types: Excitatory neurons (Ex), Inhibitory neurons (In), Astrocytes (Ast), Endothelial cells (End), Microglia (Mic), Oligodendrocytes (Oli), and Oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPC) with ArchR [19] (search distance ±500k, correlation &gt; 0.45). Assuming the distal peaks to be the enhancers, we extracted genomic sequences of the genes (promoters) and peaks (enhancers) using the reference genome hg38. In total, we adopted &gt; 1.5M pairs with each promoter sequence of 1,024 base pairs and each enhancer sequence 500 base pairs, separated by the cell types and promoters (see Table 1 and Appendix A1 1  for details).\n\n[input_representation: 2.  METHODOLOGY > 2.2. Multimodal Cell Context Instruction Tuning]\nAlthough a DNA LLM /u1D453 /u1D74D can generate DNA sequence via large-scale pretraining on a genome corpus, it is challenging for the model to understand and follow the multimodal conditions defined by cell type /u1D499 /u1D450 , gene expression /u1D499 /u1D454 , and promoter sequence /u1D499 /u1D45D . Motivated by the recent progress in multimodal LLMs [15], [16], we propose to perform postfinetuning on /u1D453 /u1D74D by leveraging the multimodal instruction tuning techniques.\n\nSpecifically, for a gene expression profile /u1D499 /u1D454 , we consider the pre-trained Geneformer single cell transcriptomes encoder [17], which provides the gene expression feature /u1D49B /u1D454 = /u1D454 /u1D499 /u1D454 ∈ ℝ /u1D451 /u1D454 . The gene embedding features before the last Transformer layer are used in our experiments. For a discrete cell type index /u1D499 /u1D450 , we utilize a trainable cell type embedding module /u1D452 /u1D753 ⋅ that maps the discrete cell type index into an embedding vector /u1D49B /u1D450 = /u1D452 /u1D753 /u1D499 /u1D450 ∈ ℝ /u1D451 /u1D452 . Since both gene expression features and cell type embeddings are in different modalities compared to DNA tokens, we apply trainable modality alignment tensors /u1D416 /u1D454 and /u1D416 /u1D452 to convert /u1D49B /u1D454 and /u1D49B /u1D450 into language embedding tokens /u1D407 /u1D454 ∈ ℝ /u1D48D /u1D454 × /u1D485 ℎ and /u1D407 /u1D450 ∈ ℝ /u1D48D /u1D450 × /u1D485 ℎ , which have the same dimensionality as the nucleotide token embedding space /u1D485 ℎ in the DNA LLM:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nWhere ⋅ represents tensor-vector product, /u1D459 /u1D454 and /u1D459 /u1D450 denote the token numbers of gene expression feature and cell type, receptively. Thus, we obtain a sequence of cell context tokens /u1D407 /u1D484/u1D461/u1D465 = /u1D407 /u1D450 /u1D407 /u1D454 . Similarly, the promoter sequence is encoded as /u1D407 /u1D45D using the word embedding of /u1D453 /u1D74D .\n\nConsequently, given the cell context tokens /u1D407 /u1D484/u1D461/u1D465 and a pair of promoter-enhancer sequence /u1D499 /u1D45D /u1D49A , the loss function that maximizes the likelihood of conditional probability /u1D45D /u1D49A /u1D499 /u1D450 /u1D499 /u1D454 /u1D499 /u1D45D is constructed as:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere /u1D73D = /u1D74D /u1D753 /u1D416 /u1D454 /u1D416 /u1D450 is the trainable parameter set.","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003188","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record describes a new model, LEONINE, for conditional DNA regulatory sequence generation using multimodal cellular data, so it is a primary model paper with a clear biological modality. The provided evidence mentions question-answering and instruction tuning, but it does not explicitly show a natural-language text input/output or a clearly substantive text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"redefining enhancer sequence design as a multimodal question-answering task within a cellular context\"; \"multimodal instruction tuning techniques\"; \"integrates multimodal cellular data-including promoter sequences, gene expression profiles, and cell type information\"; \"conditional DNA regulatory sequence generation\"; \"given the cell context tokens ... and a pair of promoter-enhancer sequence\"; \"we propose to perform postfinetuning\"; \"LLMs trained on DNA sequences\"; \"multimodal cellular data\"; \"optimize enhancer sequences\"; none"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003188","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper proposes LEONINE for conditional DNA regulatory sequence generation and explicitly describes post-finetuning a DNA LLM with multimodal context. It also states large-scale pretraining on a genome corpus and multimodal instruction tuning, which supports foundation-model-like evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"DNA LLM can generate DNA sequence via large-scale pretraining on a genome corpus\"; \"postfinetuning on... multimodal instruction tuning\"; \"conditional DNA regulatory sequence generation\"; \"generating biologically and functionally coherent sequences\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"none","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]

